\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{The dual action and a chosen bilinear self-duality}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{Let #{\rho:G\to\operatorname{Aut}_k(F)} be a
\vocabk{coefficient representation}{fdip-00SH}, with #{F}
finite free over a commutative ring #{k}. Its
\newvocab{algebraic dual action} on #{F^*} is
##{\rho^\vee(g)=(\rho(g^{-1}))^*.}
The star denotes the algebraic transpose. On the finite direct
sum of based cochain slots, evaluation identifies
#{C^\bullet(X;F)^*} with #{C^\bullet(X;F^*)}; corresponding
cell slots are paired, with no degree reversal.}

\p{A \newvocab{chosen bilinear self-duality} is a
#{G}-equivariant isomorphism #{\beta:F\to F^*}.
Its bilinear form is #{b(x,y)=\beta(y)(x)}. Equivalently,
#{b} is perfect and #{b(\rho(g)x,\rho(g)y)=b(x,y)}.
Symmetry, alternation and positivity are separate properties.
The dual action exists without choosing #{\beta}.}

\p{For the \vocabk{ring paired coefficient}{fdip-00TJ},
canonical biduality identifies #{V^{**}} with #{V}.
The \newvocab{signed paired exchange}
#{\mathcal E:\mathcal A(V^*)\to\mathcal A(V)} is the
exterior-algebra map induced by
##{V^{**}\oplus V^*\longrightarrow V^*\oplus V,\qquad
 (x,\lambda)\longmapsto(-\lambda,x).}
It swaps the two summands, with a minus sign on the primal
summand of its domain.}

\p{The algebraic dual action is the construction in Mathlib's
\leanref{Representation.dual_apply}. That declaration specifies
the representation on the dual; it makes no choice of #{\beta}.}
